= Solution
{parent=i}

Choose $R>0$ with $\operatorname{supp}\phi\subset[-R,R]$. Evenness of $f$ changes the symmetrically truncated integral into
$$
\int_{|x|>\varepsilon}\frac{f(x)}x\phi(x)\,dx
=\int_\varepsilon^R f(x)\frac{\phi(x)-\phi(-x)}x\,dx.
$$
The <mean value theorem> gives $|\phi(x)-\phi(-x)|\leq2x\|\phi'\|_\infty$. Since $f$ is continuous, it is bounded on $[0,R]$. Thus the <Cauchy principal value> exists and
$$
\boxed{\left\langle\operatorname{pv}\frac{f(x)}x,\phi\right\rangle
=\int_0^R f(x)\frac{\phi(x)-\phi(-x)}x\,dx.}
$$
This expression is linear in the <test function> and satisfies
$$
\left|\left\langle\operatorname{pv}\frac{f(x)}x,\phi\right\rangle\right|
\leq2R\max_{[0,R]}|f|\,\|\phi'\|_\infty.
$$
For each fixed <compact support>, choose one $R$ once and for all. The estimate proves that the <even-weight principal-value distribution> has <order of a distribution> at most one.

If $\phi(0)=0$, the <mean value theorem> also gives $|\phi(x)|\leq|x|\|\phi'\|_\infty$. Hence $f(x)\phi(x)/x$ is absolutely integrable near zero and has <compact support>. The truncated integrals tend to its ordinary <Lebesgue integral>, so
$$
\boxed{\left\langle\operatorname{pv}\frac{f(x)}x,\phi\right\rangle
=\int_{\mathbb R}\frac{f(x)}x\phi(x)\,dx\quad\text{if }\phi(0)=0.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**No.** Consider the continuous even <function>
$$
f(x)=\begin{cases}
0,&x=0,\\
1/\log(1/|x|),&0<|x|\leq e^{-1},\\
1,&|x|\geq e^{-1}.
\end{cases}
$$
The formulas agree at $|x|=e^{-1}$, and $f(x)\to0$ at zero. Part (i) defines its <even-weight principal-value distribution>. However,
$$
\boxed{\int_0^{e^{-1}}\frac{|f(x)|}{x}\,dx
=\int_1^\infty\frac{ds}{s}=\infty,\qquad s=\log(1/x).}
$$
If this were a <regular distribution> represented by $g\in L^1_{\mathrm{loc}}(\mathbb R)$, testing on either side of zero would force $g=f/x$ <almost everywhere> there, by uniqueness of the <locally integrable function> representing a <regular distribution>. Changing the value at zero cannot remove the displayed divergence, contradicting <local integrability> of $g$.

More generally, the exact condition is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{pv}(f/x)\text{ is regular}\iff
\int_0^r\frac{|f(x)|}{x}\,dx<\infty\text{ for some }r>0.}
$$
Necessity is the preceding argument; sufficiency follows because $f/x$ is then a <locally integrable function> and its truncated integrals converge absolutely. The value $f(0)=0$ alone provides no such rate of decay.

